# =========================================================
# TYPESCRIPT GUIDELINES
# =========================================================

## Basic Principles
- Use English for all code and documentation.
- Always declare types (no `any`).
- Always annotate function return types explicitly; do not rely on inference.
- Create types instead of abusing primitives.
- Use JSDoc for public classes/methods.
- No blank lines inside a function.
- One export per file.

## Nomenclature
- PascalCase for classes.
- camelCase for variables/functions/methods.
- kebab-case for file and folder names.
- UPPERCASE for environment variables.
- No magic numbers — define constants.
- Booleans start with verbs (isX, hasX).
- Use full words, correct spelling.
- Exceptions allowed:
  - API, URL, i, j, err, ctx, req, res, next

## Functions
- Max ~20 instructions per function.
- Single purpose functions.
- Verb-based names.
- Early returns; avoid nested blocks.
- Always use braces for all control-flow blocks (if/else/for/while/try/catch), even for single statements.
- Prefer map/filter/reduce.
- Small arrow functions (≤3 lines).
- Default param values.
- RO-RO for args & returns (object shaped).
- One abstraction level.
 - Explicit return type is REQUIRED for all functions (exported and internal).

## Data
- Prefer composite types.
- Validation inside classes (not functions).
- Immutability: `readonly`, `as const`.

## Classes
- Follow SOLID.
- Prefer composition over inheritance.
- Interfaces for contracts.
- Small classes:
  - <200 instructions
  - <10 public methods
  - <10 props

## Exceptions
- Exceptions for unexpected errors.
- Catch only to:
  - Fix expected issue or add context

## Testing
- Arrange-Act-Assert pattern.
- Clear test var names.
- Unit tests for each public function.
- Test doubles for dependencies.
- Acceptance tests per module.
- Given-When-Then pattern.

